u have  uh mentioned  the proximate cause of this is obviously  the decision to withdraw u.s troops but  you'll also know  that i've been one of many people  critical for a good number of years at  the time when troops were there was not  used to develop a stable political  settlement for the country and obviously  the great fear at the moment is that the  gains that are being  enjoyed by the people of afghanistan the  gains in education and range of other  areas even in economic development  although remains a very poor country  are on the rack i think it's premature  to say that the gains have all been  already lost  but there's clearly real danger for the  people who we are serving on the ground  today but does it tell us on that issue  of nation building that it's a fine idea  but it doesn't actually work you  mentioned iraq hasn't worked there  hasn't worked in afghanistan and in a  sense the americans  following that analysis and cutting  their losses  well the only people who can build a  nation are the citizens of the nation  but those citizens especially in poor  countries  especially in war-torn countries need  help from outside rather than hindrance  from outside i think that  the clearly the decision the military  decision which by the way is not just  american decision  america had uh about uh two and a half  thousand troops there were eight and a  half thousand nato troops ten thousand  contractors so it's quite a big  commitment that's being withdrawn  that's been decided  but the question now for all of us is  whether or not that military withdrawal  is matched by humanitarian diplomatic  political withdrawal because that really  would increase the danger just to give  you a sense of this british aid to  afghanistan is being reduced from about  90 million dollars a year down to less  than 20 million dollars  a year that's going to put  on the line some of the gains that have  been made in education in healthcare  someone who's leading a humanitarian  organization my plea would be  that those afghans on behalf of those  afghans who have gained in the last 20  years they need support from the outside  world even if it's not of a military  kind because nation building is their  business but they can't do it on their  own given the circumstances that they  face well i mean i'm just interested in  your analysis because there is such a  long history of course some the early  days overlapping some of your period as  foreign secretary was it going wrong  then what should have britain and the  americans and others have been doing  that they didn't do  well i went to afghanistan six times as  foreign secretary between 2007 and to  2010 it was one of the most dangerous  and difficult situations confronting  british foreign policy and in simple  terms i think the mistake  is as follows  the abiding approach and i'm afraid it  ran right through  to the end  was that military endeavour should then  be followed by an attempt to create a  political a stable political system  i argued from the beginning of my time  that those two things had to happen in  parallel and you asked did the troubles  start at the beginning the beginning uh  after the taliban were removed from  power in 2001 was the bonn peace  conference of 2001-2 which excluded  significant sways of the pashtun  population from the southeast of the  country and so the difficulty of  developing  politics that's based on compromise in a  country that's highly decentralized  has been an abiding problem from the  beginning of the effort that's not to  say that there hasn't been significant  gains which as i say have not yet been  erased  but i think it's very important to  learn the right lesson and my lesson is  that whether you're in the military  whether you're in diplomacy whether  you're in the humanitarian sector like  iran  none of them work without an effective  approach to a political settlement and  that can't be done sequentially military  then political they have to be done  together  so let's get to the hearing now i mean  obviously you think the americans  shouldn't be leading this withdrawal of  course britain has to go along with it  but sir the defense secretary has been  saying it is a mistake and trying to get  together some form of coalition through  nato without the americans do you think  britain should try at least to stay  well i hope you'll understand that as  someone who's got an organization with  1700 staff on the ground i'm not going  to comment on the military side of it  because that would just put them in a  danger but i want to speak to four  things that i think are really important  straight away one is the humanitarian  aid agenda this country faces covet  crisis it faces drought in 80 of the  country they need foreign aid secondly  and importantly i think it's vital  that we understand this is a regional  crisis not just a afghan crisis it  involves countries like pakistan in  countries  like iran all of which we have relations  with so that's important this has to be  dealt with at a regional level not just  a national level thirdly it's vital that  those who are in danger in the country  for example because they've served  alongside british or americans as  translators or interpreters they must be  given safe haven i think that anyone  who's in fear of their life should be  given the chance to rebuild their life  elsewhere fourthly and very importantly  there mustn't be a  political and diplomatic withdrawal  it's important that there's a retention  of the diplomatic and political muscle  inside  the country and those seem to me to be  the right priorities for  the present moment which as i say is  deadly and dangerous in all sorts of  ways just paint a picture for us of the  current trajectory if kabul falls if the  afghan government  falls the repercussions uh for  afghanistan of course and all the people  there including some of your own staff  but indeed for the entire world we're  talking many refugees many of whom will  end up in europe and potentially i've  talked about going back 20 years  could we re-establish sea re-established  a safe haven again for terrorist  groupings al-qaeda they're still around  of course and islamic state and others  well you're raising very important and  appropriate  questions you mentioned the refugee  danger that is a real and present danger  to the neighboring states rather than to  countries in europe like the uk or or  the us 30 000 refugees a week that's 100  000 refugees a month flowing into  pakistan which is already hosting 2  million  afghans and into iran which has about  800  000 so the refugee challenge is  predominantly in the region although  some will try to make it to europe as  well in fear of their  lives so there's definitely a refugee  angle it's also important to say  that afghanistan has always been subject  to pressure from its neighbors and from  great powers around it china sees  afghanistan as important pakistan for  obvious reasons sees afghanistan  as  important and for the last 20 years  western countries have worried rightly  about terrorist danger that's obviously  been degraded over the last 20 years but  that remains a reason to believe that  what starts in afghanistan doesn't say  that just want to focus for a moment on  specifically on the plight of women  there i was talking an hour ago to the  former uh women's minister in  afghanistan and many reports now  describing as the taliban retake areas  particularly young professional women  being hounded being forcibly married to  taliban fighters escaping under cover  and indeed being killed in the streets  sometimes it is a terrible situation  well i think you're absolutely right to  raise this there are two things i'd add  to what you said first of all the  greatest gains of the last 20 years have  come to those women and girls who've  been able to go to school who've been  able to get professions who've been  elected to parliament started businesses  working as teachers  so there are real gains on the rank but  afghanistan is a different country  because of those changes and because of  the aspirations and expectations of  those people secondly and vitally we  know that in all humanitarian  emergencies violence against women goes  up significantly and that's certainly  the reality that afghan women are facing  today there are staggering figures about  half of the population expecting  gender-based  violence and that's one reason that  organizations like the international  rescue committee prioritize the needs of  the women and girls who are our clients  it's another reason why i think it's so  important for the outside world to stand  with these uh women because just saying  to them you're on your own doesn't give  them the chance to work on an  on a fair playing field and so i very  much hope people  recognize that it's not just for reasons  of sacrifice that we should stand with  them it's for reasons that afghanistan  needs to be a more stable place and if  it isn't it's a threat to the region and  more widely  we very much appreciate mr milan we are  out of time your thoughts on afghanistan  but just if i may mayor squeeze in a  question about uh totally unrelated  about events in berlin today and tapping  into your deep knowledge of  international relations the discovery of  uh it seems a russian spy in the british  embassy in berlin are you surprised  weren't those days meant to be over  well i i am surprised um not because  the  danger is  not has gone away but because i thought  that we were much better at spotting the  danger but i have to plead that the  first i heard of this  i apologize was in the news headlines  just before i came on and one of the  best lessons of being foreign secretary  is not to talk about things before you  know something a bit closer to a set of  facts than a there in a question so i  hope you'll you'll  allow me to dodge that one oh the old  diplomatic skills haven't gone david  miliband great talking to you as ever  thank you very much indeed

